Forensic findings CASEID
1) Domain context: A generic institutional records office files incoming items as dated documents with a unique accession identifier, a source field, a subject classification, and a retention schedule. Documents enter through controlled intake channels (delivery desk, courier manifest, or authenticated electronic submission), are indexed against a master ledger, and are stored in the archive under the accession identifier. Access is logged per viewer with timestamp and authorization tier; alterations require a supplemental amendment document that cross-references the original accession identifier and is countersigned by a records custodian. Auditing proceeds by tracing a document's lifecycle: intake record → index entry → access log → amendment chain. Cross-document correlation (same source field across multiple accessions, or access-log clustering around a dated record) is the standard technique for reconstructing who touched what and when. This section describes filing, retention, and audit mechanics only.

2) What occurred: The actor produced a dated record (a written intake document) whose subject field described an objective related to influencing a jury member in a pending proceeding. The record referenced three component documents: (a) a candidate profile document identifying a prospective juror by a named account and enumerated attributes; (b) a contact-plan document describing a route to the juror via an intermediary, with a named account field for the intermediary; and (c) a value-transfer document specifying a payment channel, amount field, and date. The actor cross-referenced these three documents in a single accession, linked the intermediary account in the contact-plan to the recipient field of the value-transfer document, and applied a masking convention (an alias or coded identifier) to the payer field of the value-transfer document so that the source field no longer matched the actor's named account. A second dated record, submitted through a different intake channel, contained follow-up correspondence referencing the intermediary account by alias only. The actor's own named account appeared in the intake record's source field but was absent from the payer field of the value-transfer document.

3) Materials/access observed: The intake document (accession A), the candidate profile document (accession B), the contact-plan document (accession C), the value-transfer document (accession D), and the follow-up correspondence (accession E). Fields of note: source field of accession A; juror identifier and attribute fields of accession B; intermediary named-account field and alias field of accession C; payment channel, amount, date, and masked payer field of accession D; alias-only reference in accession E. Channels: a primary intake channel for accessions A–D and a secondary channel for accession E. Access log shows the actor's named account reading accessions B, C, and D on the same date as the intake of accession A.

4) Indicators & artifacts: (1) A dated record whose subject field combines a legal-proceeding reference with a named juror account. (2) A document chain where the intermediary named account in the contact-plan equals the recipient field of the value-transfer document. (3) A payer field in the value-transfer document bearing an alias that does not resolve to any source field in the ledger. (4) Two accessions (A and E) sharing a subject classification but entering through different intake channels on nearby dates. (5) An access-log cluster: one account reading the profile, plan, and transfer documents on a single date. (6) An alias appearing in two documents (C and E) with no ledger entry mapping it to a named account. (7) Absence of the actor's named account from the payer field despite presence in the source field of the root accession.